    "content": "Yes—look for **Sauerteigbrot** (“sourdough bread”) at these Munich bakeries:\n\n- **Brotatelier** — Tumblingerstraße 45, by Bahnwärter Thiel. It bakes its breads and pastries with sourdough only; the shop lists rye, spelt and wheat breads. ([brotatelier.sumupstore.com](https://brotatelier.sumupstore.com/))\n- **Julius Brantner Brothandwerk** — locations in Schwabing (Nordendstraße 23), the Altstadt (Kreuzstraße 1) and Haidhausen (Pariser Straße 38). Its range includes sourdough bread and a Saturday sourdough baguette. ([julius-brantner.de](https://julius-brantner.de/de/produkte))\n- **Bageri** — Adlzreiterstraße 21, in the Schlachthofviertel. It makes sourdough bread with Bavarian organic flour. ([bageri.de](https://bageri.de/))\n- **Hofpfisterei** — a convenient choice with many branches around Munich. Its Pfister Öko breads are made with natural sourdough; the range includes rye, mixed-grain and wholegrain loaves. ([hofpfisterei.de](https://www.hofpfisterei.de/?utm_source=openai))\n- **REIS** — Festingstraße 4 in Solln, with additional Munich locations in Forstenried and on Wolfratshauser Straße. It offers rye, mixed-rye, farmhouse and spelt sourdough breads. ([plasma.cafe](https://plasma.cafe/brot/))\n\nFor a small specialist bakery, try **Brotatelier**; for easy access across the city, **Hofpfisterei** is a good bet. Check the day’s selection before going, as some breads can sell out.",
